# 1. In:
# Hardcoded initial list of items (text string or numeric depending on context). No user input is required at run time.

# 2. Process:
# Creates a list of at least eight items related to a business field.
# Displays the whole list, extracts a chosen item by index, sorts the list in alphabetical order using sorted(), 
# and computes a aggregated metric (total/average/max length) from the collection.

# 3. Out:
# - The complete original list
# - One selected item from the list
# - The list sorted alphabetically
# - The calculated result (e.g., total marketing budget, total ad spending, or average string character length)

# 4. What my list is about, and what I computed from it:
# List Topic: E-commerce Marketing Channels & Ad Spend Budgets (in EUR).
# Computed metric: Total Monthly Marketing Budget.
# Why it is interesting: In digital marketing management, tracking total spend across active channels 
# allows managers to optimize budget allocation and calculate ROI across platforms.

# Observation Comments for "CHECK IT YOURSELF":
# Hand calculation on 3 sample channel budgets:
# Channel 1: Search Ads = 4500
# Channel 2: Social Media = 3200
# Channel 3: Email Campaign = 1500
# Sum of sample 3: 4500 + 3200 + 1500 = 9200
# Python calculation matches hand calculations exactly across all list items.

# Your code below

# List of 8 digital marketing channel monthly budgets (EUR)
marketing_channels = [
    "Search Engine Ads",
    "Social Media Ads",
    "Email Marketing",
    "Influencer Sponsorships",
    "Affiliate Marketing",
    "Display Banners",
    "Content SEO",
    "Video Campaigns"
]

budgets = [4500, 3200, 1500, 2800, 2100, 1800, 1200, 5000]

# 1. Display the whole list
print("Full List of Active Marketing Channels:")
print(marketing_channels)
print()

# 2. Display one item of your choice (e.g., item at index 1: Social Media Ads)
chosen_channel = marketing_channels[1]
print(f"Selected Channel (Index 1): {chosen_channel}")
print()

# 3. Display the list sorted alphabetically
sorted_channels = sorted(marketing_channels)
print("Channels Sorted Alphabetically:")
print(sorted_channels)
print()

# 4. Display something computed from it (Total monthly budget allocated)
total_budget = sum(budgets)
print(f"Total Monthly Marketing Budget Computed: €{total_budget:,}")